<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo I of Galicia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1228–1301</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Lev Danylovych, Lev I of Galicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, Lev Danylovych, was a son of King Daniel of Galicia and ruled the Kingdom of Galicia–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> from 1264, uniting most of its lands by 1269. He founded or developed the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, which bears his name, and moved his capital there. He cooperated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-i-of-galicia.jpg" alt="Leo I of Galicia" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Leo_I_Danilovich_-_front_side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
